WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:15.707
A container is a process with its own file system, network and limits, started from an image in a second. The Docker lab is one server with Docker Engine and Compose, and two small images already pulled.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:31.412
One cloud image; the install also pulls the images, so the exercises need no network.

00:00:31.512 --> 00:01:17.048
The engine is a daemon the docker command talks to over a socket. The lab user is in the docker group: that is why no sudo is needed.

00:01:17.148 --> 00:01:29.023
An image is the read-only template, made of layers: history shows how alpine was built.

00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:53.355
docker run starts a container from it. With --rm it is removed when the command ends; with -d it stays up: here nginx, its port 80 published on 8080.

00:01:53.455 --> 00:02:15.755
exec runs a second process inside, logs reads what the main one printed, inspect shows how it was configured. rm -f stops and removes it.

00:02:15.855 --> 00:02:35.744
A container's own layer disappears with it. A named volume survives: one container writes, a second one reads.

00:02:35.844 --> 00:03:03.763
Compose describes several containers in one file: image, ports, volumes. up -d brings them up together, down takes them away.

00:03:03.863 --> 00:03:27.742
And your own image: a Dockerfile, each instruction a layer. build it, run it, remove it.

00:03:27.842 --> 00:03:42.985
Clean again, the lab's six tests repeat every step and tell you the engine still behaves.

00:03:43.085 --> 00:03:51.200 line:6%
Every command is in the guide, in English and Italian, from the lab's card.
